Pace and time every game's presents, for the in-game settings.

The presentation hooks were development-only (--measure-native, --sample-native). They now
install with the first Metal device for every game: each drawable's presented handler
counts the frames shown and the gaps between them, and the command buffer's
presentDrawable: and presentDrawable:afterMinimumDuration: take a minimum interval, the
frame-rate cap. A present that asks for less waits at least the cap; one that asks for more
keeps its own; with no cap every present is the game's own. Where the command buffer has no
presentDrawable:afterMinimumDuration: (the simulator's), the present waits out the cap
itself. AKPresentationPacing hands both to the in-game settings in the AppKit adapter (pocket/translation/AppKit/PocketSettings.m),
which set the cap and draw the performance overlay. The presentation timestamps are asked
for by name, so a simulator drawable that has them (pocket/translation/AppKit/PocketCompat.m
adds them) is timed too. Readback and render statistics stay behind --sample-native, the
timing log behind --measure-native. Upstream candidate: brand-free; it adds one handler per
drawable and one call per present.

